i='TCS-4084';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.1: broadcast model M₂; Section 2: asynchronous, self-terminating messages and worst-case cost; Definitions 23–24, Example 25 and its final conjecture on page 26:13.'
refs += [dict(id='later',title='Refuting the Direct Sum Conjecture for Total Functions in Deterministic Communication Complexity',authors='Simon Mackenzie; Abdallah Saffidine',year=2025,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19003',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.19003v2',locator='STOC 2025 paper; first preprint 28 November 2024, version 2 revised 14 April 2026. Introduction Sections 1.1–1.2 explicitly distinguish the strong two-party conjecture from its weaker constant-factor form.'),dict(id='asymmetric',title='Consumable Data via Quantum Communication',authors='Dar Gilboa; Siddhartha Jain; Jarrod McClean',year=2024,url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2024/144/',pdf_url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2024/144/download/',locator='September 2024 report; Introduction explicitly distinguishes its shared-Alice-input asymmetric direct-sum problem from Hazan–Kushilevitz’s independent inputs.')]
notes=['Recovered the exact Ω(ℓ log n) conjecture, deterministic zero-error complexity and unrestricted interaction including messages from Alice.','Defined INBA’s ordered pair and matching-string promise; n is the bit length of a name, not the size of the universe of names.','Specified full broadcast with each transmitted bit charged once, asynchronous prefix-free messages and no free timing or speaker signaling.','Distinguished one-message-each lower bounds from the still-requested Bobs-only and unrestricted-round bounds; did not repeat the inconsistent superscript in Example 25 as a proved Bobs-only theorem.','Checked the April 2026 revision of the two-party direct-sum refutation and the 2024 asymmetric quantum report, whose models and claims do not settle this problem.']
save(i,dict(title='Unbounded-round hardness of inverted NBA',formal='Are there universal constants a>0 and n₀≥2 such that D₂(INBAₙ^ℓ)≥aℓ log₂n for every n≥n₀ and every integer ℓ≥1? Alice receives n-bit strings x₁,…,x_ℓ. Each Bobᵢ receives an ordered pair of distinct n-bit strings (uᵢ,vᵢ), with the promise xᵢ∈{uᵢ,vᵢ}. All ℓ+1 parties communicate by broadcast. Alice must output all ℓ bits bᵢ, where bᵢ=1 if xᵢ=uᵢ and bᵢ=0 if xᵢ=vᵢ. D₂ is the minimum worst-case total number of transmitted bits among deterministic protocols that are always correct, with no bound on the number of messages or turns.',definitions='''For n≥2, the names are the binary strings {0,1}ⁿ, a universe of size 2ⁿ. INBAₙ(x,(u,v)) is defined only when u≠v and x equals one of them. Its value is 1 for x=u and 0 for x=v. Thus n is a name’s bit length; log n is not the logarithm of the number of names. The superscript ℓ denotes ℓ separate promised instances, with all Alice-side inputs held by Alice and the ith pair held only by Bobᵢ. Strings and pairs may repeat between instances; there is no cross-instance distinctness or independence promise. The entire list of output bits is required, not merely whether all pairs have the same answer. Alice already knows the string xᵢ, but must learn its position in Bobᵢ’s ordered pair.

M₂ is the source’s full broadcast model. Alice and every Bob may speak, every sent message is received by all other parties, and a broadcast of b bits costs b bits total, not ℓb. Local computation, local memory and the input strings themselves are uncharged. The cost of an execution is the sum of all transmitted message lengths. The cost of a protocol is the maximum execution cost over valid promised input tuples. All outputs must be correct with zero error; no public or private random bits, nondeterminism or quantum resources are available.

Communication is asynchronous and messages are self-terminating, for example with a prefix-free encoding known to the participants. In the equivalent bit-protocol convention, a rooted binary protocol tree specifies the next speaker at each node from the common transcript, and that speaker chooses the outgoing bit using only its private input and the transcript. Alice’s final output may also depend on her private input. There is no extra input-dependent information carried for free by message length, silence, waiting time or choosing a speaker. This describes the ordinary communication-complexity convention used by the source; it is not a synchronous distributed algorithm that can signal by remaining silent in a round. Protocols halt on every promised input, but their number of turns is unrestricted and can depend on the input. Arbitrarily many alternating Alice and Bob messages are allowed.

For each n and ℓ, D₂(INBAₙ^ℓ) minimizes this worst-case bit count over all such protocols. The protocols may be chosen separately for each parameter pair; no uniform construction or efficient local algorithm is required. The proposed constants a,n₀ are independent of ℓ and n, so the lower bound is uniform in the repetition count, with no hidden restriction such as ℓ≤n. This states the source’s two-parameter Ω bound explicitly. Independent two-party computations give an O(ℓ(1+log n)) upper bound, so the conjecture asks whether interaction and broadcasting can improve that order. Bobs-only protocols forbid Alice from sending messages, and one-message-each protocols additionally restrict each Bob to one message; both are narrower than the model in the main statement.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A positive answer proves the uniform Ω(ℓ log n) lower bound against all deterministic full-broadcast protocols, including unrestricted Alice messages and arbitrarily many turns. A negative answer disproves the existence of universal constants in that bound; a constant-factor improvement of the naive cost is not by itself a refutation. A lower bound restricted to one-message-each or Bobs-only protocols is insufficient.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Why reversing the inputs matters',text='In the NBA problem Alice holds a pair and Bob holds its winning name. INBA reverses these inputs. In a two-party model one can exchange the roles, but with one Alice and many Bobs only Alice has access to all instances simultaneously, so reversing the inputs changes the multiparty distribution of information.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The known restricted-round comparison',text='Claim 21 and Example 25 establish the lower-bound comparison for one-message-each protocols. The following paragraph says that proving the analogous Bobs-only lower bound would strengthen the result, then conjectures Ω(ℓ log n) for unrestricted M₂. The card follows those explicit restrictions rather than interpreting Example 25’s inconsistent Bobs-only superscript as a proved stronger theorem.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The newer two-party refutation has different scope',text='Mackenzie and Saffidine’s STOC 2025 result, checked in its April 2026 revision, refutes the strongest direct-sum conjecture for total functions with two players. It does not analyze the inverted-NBA broadcast problem with ℓ separate Bobs. Its constant-factor savings also do not by themselves contradict an Ω(ℓ log n) claim.',citation='later'),dict(title='Shared data is a different direct-sum setting',text='The 2024 consumable-data report studies one Alice input reused across many Bob inputs and a quantum-versus-classical comparison. Its introduction explicitly distinguishes that setting from the independent Alice-side inputs in Hazan–Kushilevitz’s model.',citation='asymmetric')],why='Tests whether concentrating one side of many independent tasks at a single participant permits communication savings when everyone can hear every message. A lower bound would locate a direct-sum obstruction that survives unrestricted interaction and the reuse of broadcast information.',progress=[dict(date='2017',text='The source gives the one-message-each comparison and conjectures the uniform Ω(ℓ log n) lower bound for unrestricted deterministic M₂.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2024',text='Asymmetric direct-sum work explicitly separates its reused-data model from this multiparty distribution of independent inputs.',citation='asymmetric'),dict(date='2026-04-14',text='The checked revision of the STOC 2025 two-party refutation distinguishes the strong conjecture it refutes from weaker constant-factor direct-sum claims.',citation='later')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the broadcast definition, asynchronous communication convention, INBA promise and exact final lower-bound conjecture in the DISC paper. The source mentions a longer version, but it was not located in this review. Checked the later asymmetric report and the introduction of the April 2026 revision of the two-party refutation. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution for this exact deterministic broadcast problem; they are not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Restored all input roles and the explicit Ω bound. The n parameter measures binary string length. Lower-bound quantifiers range over all repetition counts, and unrestricted M₂ includes Alice communication. The apparent Example 25 superscript inconsistency is recorded without promoting its one-message-each result to a Bobs-only theorem.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A concrete direct-sum question separating centralized information from distributed pair inputs under unrestricted broadcast interaction. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Alice knows one n-bit name for each of ℓ tasks, while Bobᵢ knows a distinct ordered pair containing that name.','Alice must identify the name’s position in every pair.','All parties may broadcast and interact for arbitrarily many turns, with every transmitted bit charged once.','The conjecture asks whether deterministic exact protocols still need Ω(ℓ log n) total communication.','Known one-message-each bounds and later two-party direct-sum savings do not settle this unrestricted multiparty claim.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','later','asymmetric'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://acm-stoc.org/stoc2025/stoc25-program.html'])
